3. nanocode — Rust git dependencies; one crate vendored and modified
==============================================================================

Upstream:  https://github.com/nanocodexyz/nanocode
Revision:  the `rev` of the three nanocode entries in Cargo.toml
           (`NANOCODE_REV` in crates/agent-nanocode/src/lib.rs)
License:   MIT OR Apache-2.0
           (workspace `license` field in the upstream Cargo.toml at that
           revision; the upstream LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE are copied
           into vendor/nanocode-code-mode-engine/)

kata embeds nanocode's agent harness as a library: kata-agent-nanocode
depends on nanocode-backend-codex, nanocode-backend-claude and
nanocode-process-contracts, fetched from the upstream repository at that
revision, together with the nanocode crates they depend on (same revision,
same license). They are statically linked into every kata binary that
depends on kata-agent-nanocode (`cargo tree -i kata-agent-nanocode` lists
them).

Vendored and modified. vendor/nanocode-code-mode-engine/ is a copy of
nanocode's crates/runtime/code-mode-engine (the QuickJS Code Mode worker) at
that revision, with its Cargo.toml's workspace-inherited fields resolved, and
Cargo.toml's `[patch]` builds every dependency on that crate from the copy.
vendor/nanocode-code-mode-engine.patch is authored by kata and is the whole
of kata's change to it; `just nanocode-vendor-check` refuses a copy that is
anything else. The patch is described in docs/development/nanocode.md.

The engine builds QuickJS (quickjs-ng) from C through rquickjs and
rquickjs-sys (crates.io, MIT); QuickJS itself is MIT ("Copyright (c)
2017-2026 Fabrice Bellard" and others, in rquickjs-sys's quickjs/LICENSE).

