4. Other Rust dependencies (crates.io) — statically linked into kata
==============================================================================

The kata binaries statically link crates listed in Cargo.lock; the lock also
carries the dependencies only kata's tests build. Their licenses are checked by `cargo deny` against the allowlist in deny.toml:

  MIT, Apache-2.0, Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, BSD-3-Clause,
  BSD-2-Clause, ISC, Unicode-3.0, Unicode-DFS-2016, CC0-1.0, MPL-2.0, Zlib,
  CDLA-Permissive-2.0, BSL-1.0

Dependencies that need a specific mention:

  * rustls-platform-verifier (via reqwest) — kata links no root-certificate
    bundle: TLS trusts the host's certificate store. Cargo.lock still lists
    webpki-root-certs (CDLA-Permissive-2.0, Mozilla root-certificate data)
    because the verifier depends on it for wasm32 and its own tests; it is
    not linked on Linux.
  * ring (via rustls) — bundles and compiles C and assembly code under ISC,
    Apache-2.0, MIT and BSD-3-Clause terms. See its license files.
  * uluru (via gix-pack) — MPL-2.0 (file-level copyleft). kata does not
    modify its source.
  * starlark (the workflow language implementation) — Apache-2.0.

`cargo deny list` prints every crate in Cargo.lock with its license. deny.toml
does not restrict the target platforms, so that listing also names crates
that are only linked on macOS or Windows.

